{:edb/committed true :edb/basis-t 1144 :edb/replayed false :edb/tx-hash "bf65be9be868e94cca2360f4665a28131248d348677bf29ba83b283a5786588e" :edb/db-before-t 1143 :edb/db-after-t 1144 :edb/tx-data [[13194139534456 50 #inst "2026-10-07T05:27:09.153Z" 13194139534456 true] [17592186105818 1020 #edb/ref 17592186289296 13194139534456 true] [17592186105819 1020 #edb/ref 17592186289289 13194139534456 true] [17592186105819 1020 #edb/ref 17592186289317 13194139534456 true] [17592186105820 1020 #edb/ref 17592186289303 13194139534456 true] [17592186105820 1020 #edb/ref 17592186289310 13194139534456 true] [17592186289289 1017 #edb/ref 17592186289290 13194139534456 true] [17592186289289 1089 "q-147523" 13194139534456 true] [17592186289289 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045465 13194139534456 true] [17592186289289 1100 "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14009909/assets/771f1ffeda2b0041e1485611315661eed0bc88a601513d4fd4b2088022521aa8.png)\n\nConsider the linear transformation $T$ with matrix representation $T,$ given by\n\n$$\nT=[\\begin{aligned}2 & -1 \\\\ -2 & 1\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nWhich of the following is the image of the triangle shown above under the action of $T?$" 13194139534456 true] [17592186289289 1113 #uuid "708bc623-27d8-59ae-aefa-3003b8b7f233" 13194139534456 true] [17592186289289 1145 "To find the image of the triangle under the action of $T$, we first create a matrix $X$ containing all of the vertices of the triangle:\n\n$$\nX=[\\begin{aligned}0 & 1 & -1 \\\\ 2 & 1 & 0\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nNow, we compute the image of $X$ under the action of $T$ by calculating the matrix product $TX:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}TX & =[\\begin{aligned}2 & -1 \\\\ -2 & 1\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}0 & 1 & -1 \\\\ 2 & 1 & 0\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}-2 & 1 & -2 \\\\ 2 & -1 & 2\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the coordinates of the image's vertices are $(-2,2)$, $(1,-1)$,  and $(-2,2).$ Notice that all three points lie on the same line. \n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14009909/assets/e1faae8b1750b1c402f88ef801cd6609d259c37c523ac4bf4a7cb795034a9bd1.png)" 13194139534456 true] [17592186289290 1000 #edb/ref 17592186289291 13194139534456 true] [17592186289290 1000 #edb/ref 17592186289292 13194139534456 true] [17592186289290 1000 #edb/ref 17592186289293 13194139534456 true] [17592186289290 1000 #edb/ref 17592186289294 13194139534456 true] [17592186289290 1000 #edb/ref 17592186289295 13194139534456 true] [17592186289290 1040 #edb/ref 17592186045450 13194139534456 true] [17592186289290 1041 "selection" 13194139534456 true] [17592186289290 1104 #edb/ref 17592186289293 13194139534456 true] [17592186289290 1110 #uuid "775e0b0b-3551-5937-ab83-17e087f1afa2" 13194139534456 true] [17592186289291 1034 "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14009909/assets/dc6d39b05ae4862121b7b76b555be7b19931eda478e84973181e74e8d628db2b.png" 13194139534456 true] [17592186289291 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045452 13194139534456 true] [17592186289291 1111 #uuid "f3263b66-c96c-554b-8cc8-e566d2055a9b" 13194139534456 true] [17592186289292 1034 "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14009909/assets/130f215103a0c955d14ac386a382afae09c22f507491bc54a408ad13a154cbc5.png" 13194139534456 true] [17592186289292 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045452 13194139534456 true] [17592186289292 1111 #uuid "e8a9d4c9-9f57-5de7-9c26-45f26af76718" 13194139534456 true] [17592186289293 1034 "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14009909/assets/e1faae8b1750b1c402f88ef801cd6609d259c37c523ac4bf4a7cb795034a9bd1.png" 13194139534456 true] [17592186289293 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045452 13194139534456 true] [17592186289293 1111 #uuid "86e382dc-1902-5596-8db6-d162368c6dc8" 13194139534456 true] [17592186289294 1034 "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14009909/assets/f5be8864e70a021eb2fa717a9a7998c645e3792a68d23de54a07114b7a566cf1.png" 13194139534456 true] [17592186289294 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045452 13194139534456 true] [17592186289294 1111 #uuid "bd2741bf-cc00-5eed-9af1-f8df6297c68c" 13194139534456 true] [17592186289295 1034 "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14009909/assets/6b0143a0adc22d1834fc404c9da0d03b2143c8cbc16a29a9286637920694002d.png" 13194139534456 true] [17592186289295 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045452 13194139534456 true] [17592186289295 1111 #uuid "7c484290-5833-5e20-b12f-a74ffd539d46" 13194139534456 true] [17592186289296 1017 #edb/ref 17592186289297 13194139534456 true] [17592186289296 1089 "q-147670" 13194139534456 true] [17592186289296 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045463 13194139534456 true] [17592186289296 1100 "Consider the linear transformation $T$ with matrix representation $T,$ given by\n\n$$\nT=[\\begin{aligned}-5 & 2 \\\\ 10 & -4\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nA triangle $S$ has vertices at $(-2,1),(0,0)$ and $(2,2)$ Which of the following are coordinates of the vertices of ${S}^{′},$ the image of $S$ under the action of $T?$  \n\n- $(6,-18)$\n- $(-5,-20)$\n- $(12,-24)$" 13194139534456 true] [17592186289296 1113 #uuid "bb71ccfe-145d-5547-982d-cefa0d8df4a6" 13194139534456 true] [17592186289296 1145 "To find the image of $S$ under the action of $T$, we first create a matrix $X$ containing all of the vertices of $S:$\n\n$$\nX=[\\begin{aligned}-2 & 0 & 2 \\\\ 1 & 0 & 2\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nNow, we compute the image of $X$ under $T$ by calculating the matrix product $TX:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}TX & =[\\begin{aligned}-5 & 2 \\\\ 10 & -4\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}-2 & 0 & 2 \\\\ 1 & 0 & 2\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}12 & 0 & -6 \\\\ -24 & 0 & 12\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n  \n\nTherefore, the coordinates of the respective vertices of ${S}^{′}$ are  $(12,-24),(0,0)$ and $(-6,12).$ \n\nSo, the correct answer is \"III only.\"" 13194139534456 true] [17592186289297 1000 #edb/ref 17592186289298 13194139534456 true] [17592186289297 1000 #edb/ref 17592186289299 13194139534456 true] [17592186289297 1000 #edb/ref 17592186289300 13194139534456 true] [17592186289297 1000 #edb/ref 17592186289301 13194139534456 true] [17592186289297 1000 #edb/ref 17592186289302 13194139534456 true] [17592186289297 1040 #edb/ref 17592186045450 13194139534456 true] [17592186289297 1041 "selection" 13194139534456 true] [17592186289297 1104 #edb/ref 17592186289300 13194139534456 true] [17592186289297 1110 #uuid "e33b8c87-45db-5a5f-88c5-186cadf6ac06" 13194139534456 true] [17592186289298 1034 "II and III only" 13194139534456 true] [17592186289298 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045454 13194139534456 true] [17592186289298 1111 #uuid "f2477336-349a-5aac-93c2-f4350e74228a" 13194139534456 true] [17592186289299 1034 "I and III only" 13194139534456 true] [17592186289299 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045454 13194139534456 true] [17592186289299 1111 #uuid "8d64c833-fc9e-509d-83dc-ff3ea3ebafdb" 13194139534456 true] [17592186289300 1034 "III only" 13194139534456 true] [17592186289300 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045454 13194139534456 true] [17592186289300 1111 #uuid "ff0da92f-535a-53fe-90a8-68a6ef0418d2" 13194139534456 true] [17592186289301 1034 "I and II only" 13194139534456 true] [17592186289301 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045454 13194139534456 true] [17592186289301 1111 #uuid "743efd37-2195-587f-b67e-a6b6f044f636" 13194139534456 true] [17592186289302 1034 "II only" 13194139534456 true] [17592186289302 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045454 13194139534456 true] [17592186289302 1111 #uuid "b82d7844-22fd-5bff-a00d-3fd36016ed69" 13194139534456 true] [17592186289303 1017 #edb/ref 17592186289304 13194139534456 true] [17592186289303 1089 "q-162202" 13194139534456 true] [17592186289303 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045464 13194139534456 true] [17592186289303 1100 "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14009909/assets/8978d3bb02b4032b6e3a8b4a85878db647eb8323ac5b0423b5a9a6fab121c7e7.png)\n\nConsider the linear transformation $T$ with matrix representation $T,$ given by\n\n$$\nT=[\\begin{aligned}1 & 4 \\\\ -2 & -8\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nWhat would be the image of the triangle shown above under the action of $T?$" 13194139534456 true] [17592186289303 1113 #uuid "5cfe0cab-3f64-5782-9a2c-dd556be2b484" 13194139534456 true] [17592186289303 1145 "Recall that for any linear transformation $T$ with associated $2\\times 2$ matrix $T,$ we have one of the following three possibilities:\n\n- If $T$ is non-singular, i.e., $det(T)\\ne 0,$ then the image of a two-dimensional object is a two-dimensional object. In particular, the image of a polygon with $n$ vertices is a polygon with $n$ vertices.\n\n- If $T$ is singular, i.e., $det(T)=0,$ we have two additional subcases:\n\n- If $T$ has at least one nonzero entry, then the image of a two-dimensional object is a one-dimensional line segment.\n\n- If $T$ is the zero matrix, then the image of a two-dimensional object is the point $(0,0),$ the origin of the coordinate system.\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine our transformation.\n\nComputing the determinant of $T,$ we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}det(T) & =|\\begin{aligned}1 & 4 \\\\ -2 & -8\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =1\\cdot (-8)-4\\cdot (-2) \\\\  & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we have a singular nonzero matrix. Therefore, the image of the triangle under the action of $T$ is a line segment." 13194139534456 true] [17592186289304 1000 #edb/ref 17592186289305 13194139534456 true] [17592186289304 1000 #edb/ref 17592186289306 13194139534456 true] [17592186289304 1000 #edb/ref 17592186289307 13194139534456 true] [17592186289304 1000 #edb/ref 17592186289308 13194139534456 true] [17592186289304 1000 #edb/ref 17592186289309 13194139534456 true] [17592186289304 1040 #edb/ref 17592186045450 13194139534456 true] [17592186289304 1041 "selection" 13194139534456 true] [17592186289304 1104 #edb/ref 17592186289308 13194139534456 true] [17592186289304 1110 #uuid "e42fdd2c-3928-5581-8f96-17bc27665aed" 13194139534456 true] [17592186289305 1034 "The origin $O$ of the coordinate system" 13194139534456 true] [17592186289305 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045454 13194139534456 true] [17592186289305 1111 #uuid "b673724a-4915-517c-b9a0-fe79b8e7faa8" 13194139534456 true] [17592186289306 1034 "A rectangle" 13194139534456 true] [17592186289306 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045454 13194139534456 true] [17592186289306 1111 #uuid "aec1f854-7d74-50f2-a59b-e2ff15f06aa4" 13194139534456 true] [17592186289307 1034 "A triangle" 13194139534456 true] [17592186289307 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045454 13194139534456 true] [17592186289307 1111 #uuid "7b50d5f9-72ad-547c-a124-76efa4e14d33" 13194139534456 true] [17592186289308 1034 "A line segment" 13194139534456 true] [17592186289308 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045454 13194139534456 true] [17592186289308 1111 #uuid "c1bf9be5-b982-5166-9542-007bcdd73298" 13194139534456 true] [17592186289309 1034 "A point, different from the origin" 13194139534456 true] [17592186289309 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045454 13194139534456 true] [17592186289309 1111 #uuid "24847843-649b-595c-82b8-e8e87f70614a" 13194139534456 true] [17592186289310 1017 #edb/ref 17592186289311 13194139534456 true] [17592186289310 1089 "q-162208" 13194139534456 true] [17592186289310 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045463 13194139534456 true] [17592186289310 1100 "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14009909/assets/0231cc5aaf661cc873f18ff676a03d66927be48b49d38649efc45860197d18c1.png)\n\nConsider the linear transformation $T$ with matrix representation $T,$ given by\n\n$$\nT=[\\begin{aligned}2 & 0 \\\\ -1 & -1\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nWhat would be the image of the quadrilateral shown above under the action of $T?$" 13194139534456 true] [17592186289310 1113 #uuid "6bea7908-25f3-50cf-9a41-019383192d27" 13194139534456 true] [17592186289310 1145 "Recall that for any linear transformation $T$ with associated $2\\times 2$ matrix $T,$ we have one of the following three possibilities:\n\n- If $T$ is non-singular, i.e., $det(T)\\ne 0,$ then the image of a two-dimensional object is a two-dimensional object. In particular, the image of a polygon with $n$ vertices is a polygon with $n$ vertices.\n\n- If $T$ is singular, i.e., $det(T)=0,$ we have two additional subcases:\n\n- If $T$ has at least one nonzero entry, then the image of a two-dimensional object is a one-dimensional line segment.\n\n- If $T$ is the zero matrix, then the image of a two-dimensional object is the point $(0,0),$ the origin of the coordinate system.\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine our transformation.\n\nComputing the determinant of $T,$ we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}det(T) & =|\\begin{aligned}2 & 0 \\\\ -1 & -1\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =2\\cdot (-1)-0\\cdot (-1) \\\\  & =-2.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we have a nonsingular matrix. Therefore, the image of our object under the action of  $T$ is a quadrilateral." 13194139534456 true] [17592186289311 1000 #edb/ref 17592186289312 13194139534456 true] [17592186289311 1000 #edb/ref 17592186289313 13194139534456 true] [17592186289311 1000 #edb/ref 17592186289314 13194139534456 true] [17592186289311 1000 #edb/ref 17592186289315 13194139534456 true] [17592186289311 1000 #edb/ref 17592186289316 13194139534456 true] [17592186289311 1040 #edb/ref 17592186045450 13194139534456 true] [17592186289311 1041 "selection" 13194139534456 true] [17592186289311 1104 #edb/ref 17592186289315 13194139534456 true] [17592186289311 1110 #uuid "d7ad26f2-6912-513b-b733-e76e602210c0" 13194139534456 true] [17592186289312 1034 "The origin $O$ of the coordinate system" 13194139534456 true] [17592186289312 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045454 13194139534456 true] [17592186289312 1111 #uuid "0ca92b4c-bc03-577b-8a42-11e52981c0a9" 13194139534456 true] [17592186289313 1034 "A triangle" 13194139534456 true] [17592186289313 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045454 13194139534456 true] [17592186289313 1111 #uuid "0e950484-dbc4-522d-81c1-37497abf293f" 13194139534456 true] [17592186289314 1034 "A line segment" 13194139534456 true] [17592186289314 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045454 13194139534456 true] [17592186289314 1111 #uuid "fff3f9d7-1b1b-5880-b370-424998a56ba6" 13194139534456 true] [17592186289315 1034 "A quadrilateral" 13194139534456 true] [17592186289315 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045454 13194139534456 true] [17592186289315 1111 #uuid "62aaa7c9-3ff7-51da-b2b9-a1e2d05ccad8" 13194139534456 true] [17592186289316 1034 "A point, different from the origin" 13194139534456 true] [17592186289316 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045454 13194139534456 true] [17592186289316 1111 #uuid "549ca8fa-9331-5ead-a01a-20addbd7da9a" 13194139534456 true] [17592186289317 1017 #edb/ref 17592186289318 13194139534456 true] [17592186289317 1089 "q-21065" 13194139534456 true] [17592186289317 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045465 13194139534456 true] [17592186289317 1100 "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14009909/assets/db7d2b33f2badad28b754d4482047a33bb37469ed8b7b17859b10552840e2f51.png)\n\nConsider the linear transformation $T$ with matrix representation $T,$ given by \n\n$$\nT=[\\begin{aligned}-2 & -1 \\\\ 4 & 2\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nWhich of the following is the image of the triangle shown above under the action of $T?$" 13194139534456 true] [17592186289317 1113 #uuid "f28ce8d4-0044-5e40-a90c-5299f41f2c22" 13194139534456 true] [17592186289317 1145 "To find the image of the triangle under the action of $T$, we first create a matrix $X$ containing all of the vertices of the triangle:\n\n$$\nX=[\\begin{aligned}1 & 2 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 2 & 1\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nNow, we compute the image of $X$ under the action of $T$ by calculating the matrix product $TX:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}TX & =[\\begin{aligned}-2 & -1 \\\\ 4 & 2\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}1 & 2 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 2 & 1\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}-2 & -6 & -1 \\\\ 4 & 12 & 2\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the coordinates of the image's vertices are $(-2,4)$, \n$(-6,12)$ and $(-1,2).$ Notice that all three points lie on the same line.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14009909/assets/2e1dde461c6244a59672fc28c842b70a30297ac7a8c7e7cf2095a973cd1860cd.png)" 13194139534456 true] [17592186289318 1000 #edb/ref 17592186289319 13194139534456 true] [17592186289318 1000 #edb/ref 17592186289320 13194139534456 true] [17592186289318 1000 #edb/ref 17592186289321 13194139534456 true] [17592186289318 1000 #edb/ref 17592186289322 13194139534456 true] [17592186289318 1000 #edb/ref 17592186289323 13194139534456 true] [17592186289318 1040 #edb/ref 17592186045450 13194139534456 true] [17592186289318 1041 "selection" 13194139534456 true] [17592186289318 1104 #edb/ref 17592186289322 13194139534456 true] [17592186289318 1110 #uuid "3c0ee37d-c540-532c-b043-87ace79e55ef" 13194139534456 true] [17592186289319 1034 "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14009909/assets/461ff9fb5ef66239eb81314f761fac9ceb32b3bc88df6940b61445c752fe3d0c.png" 13194139534456 true] [17592186289319 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045452 13194139534456 true] [17592186289319 1111 #uuid "577bd934-421d-54ec-b0b2-d003700ec403" 13194139534456 true] [17592186289320 1034 "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14009909/assets/bf7cc4e728cd1ade741c55b2fbb54720220e0e09a8521a150300532b4e3ee9ed.png" 13194139534456 true] [17592186289320 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045452 13194139534456 true] [17592186289320 1111 #uuid "700107e3-638e-5589-9221-901c6ac2b7d9" 13194139534456 true] [17592186289321 1034 "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14009909/assets/ed8a2354f8cc04f998875f599a52b50e6c64cafe8976f1bea176772543e295ac.png" 13194139534456 true] [17592186289321 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045452 13194139534456 true] [17592186289321 1111 #uuid "1eb774ed-95c1-54d0-a9ab-0cbb1b865b7a" 13194139534456 true] [17592186289322 1034 "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference